                    <td class="py-2 pr-2" data-col="car" data-sort-value={r.car?.name ?? ''}>
                      {r.car ? <TeamLogo name={r.car.name} logoUrl={r.car.logoUrl} size="h-5 w-5" /> : <span class="text-white/30">—</span>}
                    </td>
                    <td class="py-2 pr-2" data-col="team" data-sort-value={r.team?.name ?? ''}>
                      {r.team ? <TeamLogo name={r.team.name} logoUrl={r.team.logoUrl} size="h-5 w-5" title={r.team.name} /> : <span class="text-white/30">N/A</span>}
                    </td>
                    <td class="py-2 pr-2 text-center text-white/70" data-col="start">{r.classStartingPosition ?? r.startingPosition ?? '—'}</td>
                    <td class="py-2 pr-2 text-center text-white/90" data-col="finish" data-sort-value={r.dsq ? 9999 : (r.classPosition ?? r.finishPosition)}>
                      <!--
                        The penalty asterisk is absolutely-positioned off the
                        RIGHT edge of a relatively-positioned wrapper sized
                        to the number alone, rather than sitting inline next
                        to it — an inline asterisk widens the centered
                        content every time it toggles on/off, shifting the
                        number's own visual center (same class of bug the NC
                        tag used to have here, and that ResultsTable.astro's
                        header comment describes for the driver-name case).
                        Taking the asterisk out of flow like this means
                        Tailwind's centering only ever measures the number.
                        The number itself is this driver's CLASS-relative
                        finish (classPosition), not the raw overall running
                        order — see DriverRaceHistoryRow.classPosition's doc
                        comment in results.ts for why those two differ for
                        anyone not in Alpha. data-sort-value above mirrors
                        the same number (DSQ pushed to the bottom of an
                        ascending sort via a large sentinel) so the asterisk
                        glyph itself never leaks into the sort comparison —
                        see sortable-table.ts's own doc comment for why that
                        matters.
